---
import DocsPage from '../../components/DocsPage.astro'
import { backendContract, backendOverview, backendTypesPath, sourceUrl } from '../../data/source-projection'
import { sitePath } from '../../data/site-path'
---

<DocsPage
  title="Connect your backend"
  eyebrow="INTEGRATION CONTRACT"
  summary="Keep workbook data and compute in your product while the grid stays bounded."
  alternateLocaleHref={sitePath('zh/docs/backend-port/')}
>
  <div class="docs-guide">
    <section class="docs-guide-callout" aria-label="When to implement a backend port">
      <p class="docs-guide-label">DECISION</p>
      <p>
        Use this route when your application already owns the workbook, storage, permissions, or compute runtime. If you
        only need a working Solid grid, start with the <a href={sitePath('docs/getting-started/')}>Solid quick start</a> instead.
      </p>
    </section>

    <h2>What you must implement first</h2>
    <p>The UI does not request a full workbook. A minimal editable backend has three responsibilities:</p>
    <div class="docs-guide-terms">
      <article><strong>Visible projection</strong><span>Given a window, return only its display cells.</span></article>
      <article><strong>Range projection</strong><span>Given an explicit finite range, return only that range.</span></article>
      <article><strong>Cell input</strong><span>Persist an edit, then acknowledge the bounded affected range.</span></article>
    </div>
    <pre><code>{`import type { SpreadsheetBackend } from '@einfach/spreadsheet-ui-core'

type MinimumEditableBackend = Pick<
  SpreadsheetBackend,
  'readVisibleProjection' | 'readRangeProjection' | 'setCellInput'
>`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Every other method in the contract is optional capability surface. Add it only when the product exposes its matching
      UI feature; omitted capabilities degrade by hiding or disabling that feature.
    </p>

    <h2>Implement in this order</h2>
    <ol class="docs-guide-steps">
      <li>Map the requested <code>window</code> or <code>range</code> to your storage query; never hydrate an entire sheet for a scroll.</li>
      <li>Return display-ready cells with their row, column, displayed value, and any formula or format facts your renderer needs.</li>
      <li>On <code>setCellInput</code>, make the authoritative write first. Resolve only after it lands, then return the changed range and revision.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Grow capability by capability</h2>
    <table class="docs-guide-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Add to the port</th><th>Do not do</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Large-sheet scrolling</td><td><code>readVisibleProjection</code></td><td>Send a sparse-sheet dump to the browser.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Selection and clipboard</td><td><code>readRangeProjection</code>, then export/import methods as needed</td><td>Turn selection into an unbounded read.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Editing and formulas</td><td><code>setCellInput</code></td><td>Acknowledge a write that your backend rejected.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Workbook features</td><td>Add the relevant optional method</td><td>Implement every optional capability before shipping.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <section class="docs-guide-callout docs-guide-callout-warning">
      <p class="docs-guide-label">THE BOUNDARY</p>
      <p>
        The backend owns authoritative workbook facts. The UI owns only interaction state such as selection, editing, and
        viewport. Keeping this boundary intact is what allows static, Worker, remote, and product-owned backends to share one grid.
      </p>
    </section>

    <h2>Use the source as the exact reference</h2>
    <p>
      The full interface below is extracted at build time from
      <a href={sourceUrl(backendTypesPath)}>{backendTypesPath}</a>, so it cannot drift from the implementation. The bundled
      <a href={sourceUrl('excel/solid-excel/src/adapter/static/backend.ts')}>static backend</a> is the smallest concrete reference.
    </p>
    <details class="docs-guide-details docs-guide-contract">
      <summary>Open the complete <code>SpreadsheetBackend</code> interface</summary>
      <pre><code>{backendContract}</code></pre>
    </details>
    <details class="docs-guide-details">
      <summary>Read the source-level design notes</summary>
      <pre><code>{backendOverview}</code></pre>
    </details>
  </div>
</DocsPage>
